        {
            "type": "section",
            "positioning": {
                "marginTop": 20
            },
            "elements": [
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "level": 2,
                    "content": "RF Modules",
                    "id": "modules"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    },
                    "content": "The core of the project are two components: a receiver and transmitter. I tried many different components, but the ones I landed on were these 433 MHz superheterodyne RF modules."
                },
                {
                    "type": "image",
                    "url": "https://assets.joshtreid.com/images/pi-controller/rf_modules.webp",
                    "alt": "Picture of the 433 MHz superhet RF modules. The transmitter on the left is square-shaped, and the receiver on the right is rectangular.",
                    "title": "RF Modules - Modules",
                    "screenSizes": {
                        "small": 90,
                        "medium": 70,
                        "large": 50
                    },
                    "width": 70,
                    "y": 50,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "note",
                    "level": "normal",
                    "content": "[433MHz](https://homey.app/en-us/wiki/433-mhz/) is the radio frequency used by many simple commercial applications like garage doors, lights, lamps, smart switches, etc. This project specifically targets 433 MHz only.",
                    "width": 80
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "content": "Receiver"
                },
                {
                    "type" :"text",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    },
                    "content": "The receiver is the longer, rectangular module. The receiver uses ASK modulation and outputs a plain bit stream. The ESP32 takes this and uses one of two methods that the user has to select. The first method uses the RC-Switch library that attempts to recognize the signal and ouput a protocol, pulse length, bit count, and integer value."
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "language": "cpp",
                    "filename": "esp32.ino",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    },
                    "content": "mySwitch.enableReceive(RX_PIN);\n\nunsigned long deadline = millis() + 5000;\nwhile (millis() < deadline) {\n    if (mySwitch.available()) {\n        long val   = mySwitch.getReceivedValue();\n        int  bits  = mySwitch.getReceivedBitlength();\n        int  proto = mySwitch.getReceivedProtocol();\n        int  dly   = mySwitch.getReceivedDelay();\n        mySwitch.resetAvailable();\n\n        piSerial.println(String(\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"success\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"recRC\\\",\\\"protocol\\\":\") + proto + \",\\\"delay\\\":\" + dly + \",\\\"bits\\\":\" + bits + \",\\\"value\\\":\" + val + \"}\");\n        mySwitch.disableReceive();\n        return;\n    }\n}\n\nmySwitch.disableReceive();"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "piSerial.println() prints to the Raspberry Pi, which saves the remote as: "
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "language": "json",
                    "filename": "remotes.json",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    },
                    "content": "{\n    \"name\": \"LampON\",\n    \"type\": \"rc_remote\",\n    \"protocol\": 1,\n    \"delay\": 387,\n    \"bits\": 24,\n    \"value\": 7869186\n},"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "The second method records the raw timing between when the signal altermates between HIGH and LOW. This can then be replayed by the transmitter, allowing the device to copy remotes that can't be decoded by the RC-Switch library."
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "language": "cpp",
                    "filename": "esp32.ino",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    },
                    "content": "rmt_item32_t* captured = nullptr;\nint capturedCount = 0;\n\nunsigned long deadline = millis() + 5000;\n\nwhile (millis() < deadline) {\n    size_t rx_size = 0;\n    rmt_item32_t* items =\n        (rmt_item32_t*)xRingbufferReceive(rb, &rx_size, 50 / portTICK_PERIOD_MS);\n\n    if (items) {\n        int pulses = rx_size / sizeof(rmt_item32_t);\n\n        if (pulses >= MIN_PULSES) {\n            capturedCount = min(pulses, MAX_RAW);\n            captured = (rmt_item32_t*)malloc(capturedCount * sizeof(rmt_item32_t));\n            if (captured) memcpy(captured, items, capturedCount * sizeof(rmt_item32_t));\n            vRingbufferReturnItem(rb, items);\n            break;\n        }\n        vRingbufferReturnItem(rb, items);\n    }\n}"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "Each timing is recorded and the sign it is recorded represents HIGH or LOW"
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    },
                    "language": "json",
                    "filename": "remotes.json",
                    "content": "{\n    \"name\": \"OverheadON3\",\n    \"type\": \"raw_remote\",\n    \"code\": [\n        3933,\n        -4078,\n        747,\n        -465,\n        141,\n        -1047,\n        161,\n        -423,\n        776,\n        -432,\n        771,\n        -436,\n        768,\n        -431,\n        178,\n        -1016,\n        186\n}"
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "Transmitter",
                    "size": 2,
                    "level": 3
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    },
                    "content": "The smaller, square-ish model is the transmitter. The ESP32 takes the remote, either `raw_remote` or `rc_remote` and uses either its protocol/delay/value/bit or the raw timings and sends the correct pulses to the transmitter to act like the remote. In combination with the receiver, these two components allow you to save and replay 433 MHz remotes as long as the device doesn't use a security algorithms like rolling codes."
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "section",
            "positioning": {
                "marginTop": 20
            },
            "elements": [
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "level": 2, 
                    "content": "Circuit Board",
                    "id": "circuit"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "Regardless if using the RC-Switch libary or the RMT hardware, recording and replaying remotes require controlling pulse timings with microsecond precision. To achieve this, I chose to use an ESP32 in between the Raspberry Pi and the RF modules. To keep everything together, I designed and ordered a PCB."
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "content": "Schematic"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    },
                    "content" :"The first-ish step was to crate a schematic in KiCAD. The schematic is used for definining the underlying circuit logic. It defines what components or pins are connected to what and how everything is powered. In the schematic are the 2x20 pin header for the Raspberry Pi, the ESP32, and the RF modules. There are also resistors for dropping the 5V receiver output to 3.3V, and decoupling capcatitors to stabilize local supply voltages."
                },
                {
                    "type": "image",
                    "url": "https://assets.joshtreid.com/images/pi-controller/schematic.webp",
                    "alt": "Schematic of the PCB for the project",
                    "title": "Circuit Board - Schematic",
                    "screenSizes": {
                        "small": 90,
                        "medium": 70,
                        "large": 50
                    },
                    "width": 90,
                    "y": 50,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "note",
                    "level": "warning",
                    "content": "There is limited information on the modules. Some of the information, such as votlages, come from the reviews, so take this schematic with a grain of salt.",
                    "width": 80
                },
                {
